  "query_preview": "..\n\nUpstream MCS now entering Indiana will continue to progress east \nthrough the evening. Convective activity will stay locked to the \nCAPE gradient which resides across central Indiana and trails down \ninto southwest Ohio. However, the associated stratiform region which \nextends through southern Lake Michigan will attempt to work across \nthe southern portion of the cwa through the evening. A modest dry \n10kft dry layer in the low to mid levels of the atmosphere will work\nto erode northern periphery of the ongoing stratiform rain, but \nthere looks to be enough saturation to overcome this layer closer to \nthe MI/OH border. The chance for this drizzle or light rain will \nextend north to M59, but the more favorable probabilities are hedged \nto the south. Some isolated rumbles of thunder cannot be ruled out \nnoting elevated cape value aob 500 J/kg, but overall cape density is \npoor with a weak and elevated cap around 20 kft. Any prolonged \nactivity is expected to hold just along or south of the state line, \nmainly across the Ohio Valley. \n\nLimited forcing under diffuse high pressure brings mostly dry \nconditions tomorrow. The exception for very low end chances will \nagain reside closer to the MI/OH border if the northern periphery of \nprecipitation from low-level convergence encroaches into the cwa. \nIncreasing shower and thunderstorm chances enter on Saturday with \nthe best chances centered Saturday evening to Sunday morning. \nInitial shower and storm chances will be tied to lift along a warm \nfront that will push through in the late afternoon to evening hours. \nW-E re-orientation of the llj will rapidly enhance moisture \nadvection, increasing pw values aoa 2\" by Saturday evening with 850mb dew \npoints nearing 18C. Any subtle forcing will be capable of producing \nshowers or storms, but any more widespread activity would likely be \ntied to maintenance and progression of a mcs originated from the \nPlains. Passage of this system and area of convectively enhanced low \npressure will work to push a weak cold front across the Great Lakes \non Monday. This will afford a window for drier conditions Monday-\nTuesday along with slightly cooler temperatures with highs in the low\n80s.",
